<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Princeps</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Princeps, meaning first citizen, was the title adopted by Augustus to describe his position after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. By avoiding the title of king, he maintained the appearance of republican government while holding supreme power. The term gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principate">Principate</a>, the early form of rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The transition from Republic to Empire was therefore not a formal revolution but a constitutional fiction: Augustus styled himself <b>princeps</b> (first citizen) rather than rex (king), a distinction that proved politically essential in a culture that had expelled its last king, Tarquinius Superbus, nearly five centuries earlier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pelagius's victory at Covadonga in 718 began the Reconquista, and the Kingdom of Asturias traced itself to a Visigothic nobleman elected <b>princeps</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Lawyers drew on Roman law, whose maxim <b>princeps</b> legibus solutus est, “the prince is released from the laws”, matched the Latin absolutus, “released” or “freed”, from which “absolute” comes; the noun absolutism is a much later coinage.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Scheil published the editio <b>princeps</b> in 1902, with a transliteration and a free French translation, and editions followed in German by Hugo Winckler in 1902, in English by Claude Johns and in Italian by Pietro Bonfante in 1903.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The first printed edition, the editio <b>princeps</b>, was edited by Demetrios Chalkokondyles and published in Florence in 1489 by Bernardus Nerlius and Demetrius Damilas.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperor was in name the “first citizen” (<b>princeps</b>) and in fact the master of the state.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-rome">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Rome</a></li></ul></section>
